{
  "id": "inverted-u-hypothesis",
  "term": "inverted-U hypothesis",
  "definition": "The inverted-U hypothesis is the idea that a response increases as complexity rises to a moderate level, then decreases when complexity becomes too low or too high. In this music-psychology context, it refers to the claim that rhythms with medium complexity produce a stronger pleasurable urge to move than very simple or very complex rhythms.",
